Task 1: Scheduling a One-Time Backup Task
Subtask 1.1: Create a Simple Backup Script
Create a backup directory:

mkdir ~/backups
Create a backup script:

nano ~/backup_script.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash
echo "Backup started at $(date)" >> ~/backups/backup_log.txt
tar -czf ~/backups/home_backup_$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz ~/Documents
echo "Backup completed at $(date)" >> ~/backups/backup_log.txt
Make the script executable:

chmod +x ~/backup_script.sh
Subtask 1.2: Schedule the Backup with at
Schedule the backup to run in 5 minutes:

at now + 5 minutes -f ~/backup_script.sh
Press Ctrl+D to save the job
Verify the scheduled job:

atq
Expected Output: Shows your scheduled job with job ID

Subtask 1.3: Alternative Time Formats
Try different scheduling formats:

at 11:30 PM tomorrow
at 9:00 AM next week
at now + 1 hour
Task 2: Managing Scheduled Tasks
Subtask 2.1: List Scheduled Jobs
View all pending jobs:
atq
Expected Output: Lists job ID, date/time, and queue
Subtask 2.2: Remove a Scheduled Job
Identify the job ID from atq output
Remove a specific job:
atrm <job_id>
Subtask 2.3: View Job Details
For advanced users: View the contents of a scheduled job
sudo ls /var/spool/at
sudo cat /var/spool/at/<job_file>
Troubleshooting Tips
If at commands don't work:
Verify atd service is running: systemctl status atd
Check system logs: journalctl -u atd
Permission issues:
Ensure your user is in /etc/at.allow (or not in /etc/at.deny)
Timezone problems:
Verify system time: timedatectl
